{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1852178230/6/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"8886d717\" lines=\"43-63\">\n<description>\nRepair markers embedded in the main object and copy the remaining markers one pixel toward the solid edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- A mostly background grid contains one solid non-background edge line.\n- Apart from that edge and the background, there is one large main object\n  color and a smaller marker color.\n- Each marker is either embedded in the main object, touching it on at\n  least three 4-neighbor sides, or isolated on the background.\n\nRule:\n- Embedded markers are recolored to the main object color.\n- Isolated background markers remain in place and also paint the adjacent\n  background pixel in the direction of the solid edge line.\n- All other pixels are unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\nThe full edge line is a direction cue: it tells which way the background\nmarkers extend, while markers surrounded by the main object are simply\ndefects to be filled.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"522fdd07\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nCycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.\n- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- Keep each square's color and center.\n- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"97c75046\" lines=\"88-107\">\n<description>\nMove the single marker to the endpoint reached by sliding along the obstacle contour.\n\nInput structure:\n- A dominant background color fills the canvas.\n- One single-pixel marker sits away from a connected obstacle region of the\n  remaining color.\n- The marker has exactly one horizontal or vertical sightline to the obstacle.\n\nRule:\n- Find the last background pixel before the obstacle along that sightline.\n- From that pixel, try the two diagonal turns that continue around the\n  obstacle boundary.\n- Move the marker to the endpoint of the turn that remains 4-adjacent to the\n  obstacle for the longest distance.\n\nKey insight:\nThe marker is not placed by distance to the obstacle; it first points to the\nobstacle, then follows the visible outside contour until that contour segment ends.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c61be7dc\" lines=\"42-57\">\n<description>\nFlatten the interior object into a centered line and move the guide bars inward to bracket it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- One non-background guide color touches the boundary and forms exactly two full parallel bars plus a split perpendicular center bar.\n- The other non-background color is a single 8-connected interior object whose area determines the flattened line length.\n\nRule:\n- If the guide pair is vertical, make a vertical object-colored line centered between those bars; otherwise use the analogous horizontal line.\n- The line length equals the number of interior object pixels and is centered on the split perpendicular guide bar.\n- Rebuild the guide color as the perpendicular center bar plus two full bars immediately beside the flattened object line.\n\nKey insight:\n- The guide bars supply only the orientation and center; the interior object's pixel count supplies the length of the final one-cell-thick line.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d753a70b\" lines=\"81-93\">\n<description>\nRed diamond outlines contract by one layer and gray diamond outlines expand by one layer while other objects stay fixed.\n\nInput structure\n- A 16x16 orange-background grid contains one-cell-thick Manhattan-diamond outlines.\n- Red and gray outlines are the changing objects; other colored diamond outlines are static.\n- Some outlines may be clipped by the grid boundary, but their visible pixels still fit a clipped Manhattan-distance contour.\nRule\n- Clear all red and gray pixels.\n- Red objects are redrawn with the same diamond center and radius minus one.\n- Gray objects are redrawn with radius plus one; an ambiguous edge-clipped growing arc continues away from the nearest perpendicular edge.\nKey insight\n- The task is not about moving whole objects: each changing object is a contour of a diamond, and the visible operation is one step of contour contraction or expansion.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"d749d46f\" lines=\"32-51\">\n<description>\nPack each solid foreground rectangle twice: wide across the top and tall from the bottom.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has one background color and one foreground color; the foreground forms\nseparate solid rectangles, ordered from left to right.\n\nRule\nBuild a 10-row background canvas whose width is the packed width of the wide\nversions of the rectangles. For each source rectangle, paint a top-aligned\nrectangle of shape min(h,w) by max(h,w), with one background column between\nconsecutive rectangles. Then, starting again from the left edge, paint a\nbottom-aligned rectangle of shape max(h,w) by min(h,w) for each source rectangle,\nagain separated by one background column.\n\nKey insight\nThe input rectangles are not copied in their original orientations; each object\ncontributes one horizontal-facing and one vertical-facing normalized rectangle,\npreserving left-to-right object order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"42f14c03\" lines=\"75-83\">\n<description>\nCrop the foreground object whose missing cells match marker shapes, and fill those missing cells with the marker colors.\n\nInput structure\nA dominant background surrounds several monochromatic foreground objects. Exactly one object has background gaps inside its bounding box whose component shapes are matched by separate marker objects.\nRule\nFind that object, crop its bounding box, and replace each background gap component with the color of the separate marker component or components having the same normalized shape.\nKey insight\nThe marker objects do not mark absolute positions; their shapes identify which gaps in the selected object should be colored, while objects with unmatched gaps are distractors.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2e65ae53\" lines=\"88-103\">\n<description>\nFill every lattice slot from the color assigned to that slot position by the other lattices.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The largest foreground color forms several one-pixel-thick rectangular lattice frames.\n- The frame lines divide each lattice into the same row-by-column set of rectangular slots; some slots already contain one non-frame color.\n\nRule:\n- Infer a color for each logical slot position from the colored slots across all lattice frames.\n- Repaint every slot interior in every lattice with the inferred color for its row/column position.\n- Keep the frame lines and all background outside frames unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- Slot sizes vary by frame, but the complete color pattern is indexed by lattice slot position rather than by absolute pixel size or location.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"fc7cae8d\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nAlign a central rectangle to two colored frame-edge controls via rotation/reflection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg grid with two solid-colored border edges (distinct non-bg colors).\n- A central filled rectangle with 1px colored guide edges on two sides, colors matching the frame controls.\n\nRule:\n- Rotate/mirror the object so its guide edges face the same sides as the matching frame controls.\n- Crop off the 1px guide edges from aligned sides.\n\nKey insight: Guide-edge colors encode target frame sides; the correct dihedral transform matches both simultaneously.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"291dc1e1\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nReorder and stack rectangles by proximity to two colored edge lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two edge lines (h + v) meet at a corner marked by a unique color.\n- \"Blue\" line is color 1 if present, else the longer line; other is \"red.\"\n- Solid rectangles in the interior.\n\nRule:\n- Sort rectangles by (distance to blue edge, distance to red edge).\n- If the blue edge is vertical, rotate each rectangle 90° CCW.\n- Stack top-to-bottom, centered horizontally.\n\nKey insight: Edge-line colors encode a coordinate system; proximity to each axis determines stacking order and rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b7256dcd\" lines=\"18-32\">\n<description>\nTransfer each colored marker to its adjacent neutral mask component, then erase the markers.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the most common color.\n- The neutral mask color is the unique most frequent foreground color.\n- Other foreground colors are marker pixels that may touch neutral 4-connected mask components.\nRule\n- Remove every marker pixel by recoloring it to the background.\n- For each 4-connected neutral mask component, find marker colors touching it by edge adjacency.\n- If a component touches one marker color, recolor the whole neutral component to that marker color.\n- If a component touches no marker color, leave it neutral.\nKey insight\n- Marker pixels label neighboring neutral regions but are not part of the final colored objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\" lines=\"42-53\">\n<description>\nRotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\nRule\n- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\nKey insight\n- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6bcdb01e\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nExtend the edge-touching green start bar through the corridor, turning at each teal blocker until the path leaves the grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the most common color.\n- Exactly two foreground colors are present: a 2-pixel 4-connected start bar and many blocker pixels.\n- Exactly one pixel of the start bar lies on the grid border.\n\nRule:\n- Start from the border pixel of the bar and move through the other bar pixel into the grid.\n- Paint every traversed non-blocker pixel with the start color.\n- When the next cell is a blocker, turn to the unique perpendicular non-blocker neighbor; when the next cell is outside the grid, stop.\n\nKey insight: The blocker pixels define a one-cell-wide corridor, so every interior collision has exactly one valid 90-degree turn.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"996ec1f3\" lines=\"16-31\">\n<description>\nCompress the noisy four-region board split by a solid cross into a 3x3 color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- A single full row and a single full column have the same solid separator color.\n- The separator cross divides the input into four nonempty rectangular regions.\n- Each region has a unique dominant color, with other colors acting as noise.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
